This is the **1/3–2/3 conjecture** (also known as the Fishburn–Trotter conjecture), a well-known **open problem** in combinatorics that has remained unsolved since it was posed in the late 1960s. The Lean formalization itself tags it with `@[category research open]`, confirming its open status.

The conjecture asserts that every finite partially ordered set which is not a total order contains two elements $x, y$ such that the probability that $x$ precedes $y$ in a uniformly random linear extension lies in the interval $[\tfrac{1}{3}, \tfrac{2}{3}]$.

Despite significant effort and partial results (e.g., it is known to hold for certain classes of posets, and weaker bounds are established in general), no complete proof is known. A full resolution would be a major advance in order theory and would likely be published in a top mathematics journal.

